# etude-pqv plan — machine-readable seat exceptions and regeneration safety

ROLE: Review the PLAN phase for etude-pqv.

ANCESTOR: etude-pqv (P3, urgent dependency of etude-9uf) — replace the observed Opus nested-Claude 401 comment-only exception and prevent `etude init --force` from regenerating stale seat behavior. MEASUREMENT: the committed registry currently differs semantically from `registry.Default()` in four seats/settings, and the known nested `claude -p` path returns HTTP 401 under a Claude Code orchestrator.

BLAST RADIUS: none: worktree and repo only. The plan changes the registry schema/default/scaffold, focused registry and init migration tests, and shipped registry documentation. It does not edit the 9uf-owned gate implementation, dogfood plans/scripts, or host configuration.

IRREVERSIBLE SEQUENCES: none

TOPOLOGY: single-process parsing and ordered caller-directed retry. No concurrent mutation or ambient-orchestrator discovery is introduced.

DURABILITY CLASS: committed Git configuration and compiled defaults are durable; candidate enumeration is read-only and each candidate is attempted at most once per gate round. There is no delivery queue or generation identity.

COMPLETENESS ENUMERATION: the complete schema/default surface is `Seat`, `SeatInvocation`, validation, YAML decode, YAML encode, `Default()`, the checked-in registry, shipped registry docs, and semantic-equality regressions. The candidate-enumeration helper is a single-site API; engine call sites are intentionally not changed in this bead. `model_fallbacks` remains an ordered set of alternate model identifiers within the primary harness; `invocation_fallbacks` is the complete ordered set of alternate harness invocations.

TRUST BOUNDARY: `.etude/registry.yaml` is trusted operator-authored configuration parsed with unknown-field rejection. A consumer may retry only the explicit ordered candidates returned by the registry; ambient environment variables are untrusted input for selecting candidates and are never consulted.

## Decision on item (1)

The live-run engine will NOT auto-detect or mechanize the Claude-orchestrator exception. The engine is an external process with no authenticated in-harness Task primitive, and ambient environment is not a trustworthy orchestrator identity (this Codex session itself carries both Codex and Claude-related variables). Auto-detection could silently select the wrong harness and misreport producer/session metadata. Retry policy and gate-round accounting remain the gate consumer's responsibility.

Instead, make the exception explicit and machine-actionable as an ordered invocation fallback at the registry/consumer boundary:

- Add `Seat.InvocationFallbacks []SeatInvocation`, encoded as `invocation_fallbacks`.
- `SeatInvocation` carries the alternate `harness`, `invoke`, and optional `mode`.
- Add `Seat.Invocations() []SeatInvocation`, which returns the primary invocation followed by configured fallbacks in order. Consumers such as the arriving `etude gate` command attempt each candidate at most once under their existing retry/round policy.
- The canonical Opus fallback uses the already-approved executable substitute `agy --model opus --print`, with harness `agy`. This gives a CLI consumer an executable fallback after `claude -p` fails; the existing review skill may continue selecting the stronger authenticated in-harness Task path before invoking a CLI candidate when that primitive exists.
- `model_fallbacks` is not an existing solution to the measured failure: it carries model identifiers only and cannot change the unavailable `claude` harness. The two fallback lists have distinct, documented roles.

This is deliberately explicit ordered fallback data, not engine heuristics. The decision and rationale will also be appended to the bead notes so the etude-9uf worker has an authoritative answer.

## Regeneration-safe default

- Carry every current machine-relevant `.etude/registry.yaml` setting into `registry.Default()`: the `dev` seat required by the default workflow runner, codex xhigh/read-only invocation and fallback list, Gemini project-env unsets/skip-trust invocation and fallback list, tier `use` prose, plus the structured Opus invocation fallback.
- Replace the comment-only exception in `.etude/registry.yaml` with `invocation_fallbacks`. Comments may still explain intent, but no required behavior depends on comment preservation.
- Add a semantic equality regression between parsed `.etude/registry.yaml` and `registry.Default()`. This makes future scaffold drift fail tests and proves `etude init --force` regeneration preserves effective settings even though YAML comments are not round-tripped.

## Validation and compatibility

- Require non-empty `harness` and `invoke` for every invocation fallback; `mode` remains free text in this bead because closed-set mode validation belongs to etude-zdl.
- Unknown fallback fields remain rejected through yaml.v3 KnownFields.
- Existing registries without `invocation_fallbacks` remain valid and round-trip unchanged.

